.he "kstat (1)                    9/30/26                    kstat (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
kstat
- dump the kernel's tables
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
kstat
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Kstat
reports the kernel's internal tables as they stand at the moment it
runs, by reading them out of the machine's memory.  Where ps answers
"what is running", kstat answers "what is the kernel holding" - the
buffer cache, the open files, the inodes in core, the mount table, and
the terminal structures behind a terminal that has stopped answering.
.sp
Two files are read and they are read differently.  The addresses come
from
.bd
/micronix,
the kernel object file the boot loader loads: kstat reads its symbol
table and takes each table's address from the symbol that names it.
The contents then come from
.bd
/dev/mem
at those addresses.  So the tables printed are the live ones, and a
table whose symbol is not in the kernel is skipped without a word - no
error, no heading and no line to say it was passed over.
.sp
The field offsets are the kernel's by construction, because the
struct layouts come from the same headers the kernel is built with.
That is what makes the dump readable at all, and it is also the one
thing to keep in mind when reading it: a kernel whose structs have
changed needs kstat rebuilt, not merely rerun.
.sp
There are no arguments and no options.  Everything kstat knows how to
print, it prints.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
WHAT IT PRINTS
.in +5
.br
The sections come in this order.
.sp
.in +5
.ti -5
config	The root and swap devices, the swap area's size and starting
	block, and then the addresses of _nbuf, _nbdev and _ncdev.
	Those last three are printed as addresses rather than as
	values, so they are where those variables live and not what
	they hold - the count of each is reported by the section that
	uses it.
.sp
.ti -5
process table	One line per allocated entry of _plist: its slot and
	address, pid, the eight characters of args, mode decoded into
	flag names, uid, priority, nice value, sleep event, tty
	number and parent.  A second line carries status, nsegs,
	time, alarm and the swap address.  This is ps's table with
	the user structure left out, and with the fields ps has no
	column for.
.sp
.ti -5
tty structures	One per mult I/O port, four of them.  Each shows its
	device number, input and output speed, erase and kill characters,
	mode and state decoded, column, line, nextc and nbreak, then
	its three queues - raw, canonical and output - with the
	address of each, its count, and its first and last link.  It
	ends with the open count, mstate decoded, and start, stop,
	put and set.  This is the section that explains a terminal
	that has stopped responding: a queue with a count that never
	drops, or a first link that never advances, is where the
	characters went.
.sp
.ti -5
buffer headers	One line per buffer, the count from _nbuf: device,
	block number, flags decoded, count, data address, xmem,
	error, time, then the forward and back links, the cylinder
	and the hash.  A header filed under a hash it does not
	belong under is what a bget() that never returns looks like
	from here.
.sp
.ti -5
mount table	One line per mounted filesystem still in use: device, the
	root inode's number, read-only, and the sizes of the free
	list and the inode list.
.sp
.ti -5
file table	One line per open file: mode, reference count, inode
	number, and the read/write pointer.  The pointer is a 32-bit
	offset printed as its two 16-bit words, most significant
	first, which is the Z280's word-swapped long and not a pair
	of numbers to read as one.
.sp
.ti -5
inode table	One line per in-core inode in use: device, number, mode,
	link count, uid, gid, size, flags decoded, reference count,
	the mount table slot it belongs to, and the time.
.sp
.ti -5
clist	The number of free cblocks, counted by walking the free list
	from _cfree.  If the list loops it says so and stops rather
	than spinning, and it names the count it reached.  A cblock
	free list that loops is exactly the fault this counter
	exists to catch.
.sp
.ti -5
device switches	The block and character switch tables, _biosw and
	_ciosw: three words per block entry and five per character.
	These are the handlers' addresses and nothing is decoded.
	Compare one against a symbol to find which driver a major
	number reaches.
.sp
.in -5
.sp
A field whose bits are none of the ones a table knows prints a single
.bd
-
so that the field is never blank and its position in the line still
says which field it is.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
kstat writes its errors to the standard output rather than the
standard error, each one prefixed with
.bd
kstat:.
It exits 0 after a dump, and 1 only when
.bd
/dev/mem
cannot be opened - so, unlike
.bd
ps (1),
whose status is inverted, kstat's status is the one a shell expects.
.sp
kstat is not linked with the C library.  It declares for itself what
it wants of lseek, and there the declaration matters as much as the
call: without it the compiler assumes an int return, keeps the low
word of a file position, and reads the wrong symbol.  The symbol table
sits past the 64K a word-sized offset reaches, so every symbol lookup
depends on that declaration being there.
.sp
The number of mult I/O ports is written into kstat rather than read
from a header, because the kernel keeps it in a source file and does
not publish it.  If the kernel gains or loses a port, kstat's copy has
to be changed by hand to match.
.sp
kstat reads the kernel's symbol table, so it names kernel symbols and
will print a table for any symbol it finds.  It has no notion of a
kernel that is not the one it is running on: pointed at a stale
.bd
/micronix
it will read the live memory at the stale addresses and print
something confidently wrong.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/bin/kstat
.br
/micronix
.br
/dev/mem
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
